  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 AM EDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nIt's a quiet night across eastern TN/southwestern VA/southern NC. \nThe majority of this evening's convective activity has occurred off \nto the south and east of our forecast area. For tonight, \ntemperatures cool into the low to mid 60s under mostly clear skies. \nWinds are light to calm and we could see areas of fog develop near \nthe regions waterways. Any fog that does form will quickly burn off \nafter sunrise Saturday morning. \n\nOur weather pattern over the next week or so will be dominated by an \nupper level ridge feature that'll keep us hot, humid, and mostly \ndry. Any afternoon convection that does fire should form along the \nSmokies and drift east into the Carolinas. This upper level ridge \nwill do a few things for us. First, we'll see warmer temperatures \nreturn. Highs in the 90s each day with some locations seeing \ntemperatures in the mid to possibly upper 90s in SE TN. Second, \nit'll usher in higher surface dew points and that'll combine with \nthe warmer temperatures to support higher heat indices in the 100 to \n105 range each day. Lastly, outside of the Smokies it'll be pretty \ndry. While this ridge is strong, it isn't immune to the train of \nshortwave troughs guidance brings through over the next week or so. \nLooking ahead, there is some signal to support a stronger trough \njust in time for next weekend. That could help nudge this ridge back \nto the west and allow for slightly cooler and drier air to return. \nHowever, that's over a week away and guidance doesn't handle these \ntypes of upper level patterns too good imho. For now, sit back and \nenjoy the end of Summer.",
